  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "The name of Fourche derives from the French word fourche, meaning 'fork,' a reference to the branching of the Fourche La Fave River, which flows through the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fourche_arkansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Fourche's early 1900s saw fierce lumber rivalries between Bryant Lumber and Fourche River Lumber, sparking lawsuits, arson, and dynamite attacks on railroad tracks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fourche_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the Civil War, German immigrants like Gottlob Faisst opened a tomato cannery, wine saloon, and Bryant Lumber Company in Fourche.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fourche_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1913, Bryant Lumber faced federal peonage charges for hiring Hungarian workers, but the company was acquitted after proving contractual agreements.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fourche_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community also included early African American residents, with Baptist and Methodist churches and a school that operated until the mid-1950s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
